      "q": "2. Fishing\n: I have also seen fishermen catching fish from rivers and seas. The natural resources used in this activity are water bodies and fish. Fishermen rely on clean water and the availability of fish for their livelihoods.\nLET\u2019S EXPLORE\nNow that we have seen some examples of secondary sector activities, can you name two more economic activities in the secondary sector?",
      "a": "Here are two more economic activities in the secondary sector:\nTextile Manufacturing:\nThis involves turning raw materials like cotton or wool into fabric and clothing through processes like spinning, weaving, and dyeing.\nCar Manufacturing:\nThis involves assembling various parts and materials such as steel, rubber, and glass to produce cars in factories.\nThese activities in the secondary sector focus on processing raw materials into finished goods for consumers.\nTHINK ABOUT IT\nObserve the different stages of the process shown in Fig. 14.1 on page 206 and discuss them with your fellow classmates.\nAns:\nIn\nFig. 14.1\n, we see a sequence that represents different stages of production, starting from the extraction of raw materials to the final sale of a product. Let\u2019s break down the stages:\nTree Cutting\n: This represents the primary activity of cutting down trees, which is part of the\nPrimary Sector\n. The natural resource here is wood.\nTransporting Logs\n: After cutting the trees, the logs are loaded and transported to a factory. This involves logistics and falls under the\nTertiary Sector\n(services).\nProcessing Logs\n: In this stage, the logs are processed in a factory, where they are turned into usable products like paper. This is part of the\nSecondary Sector\nas it involves manufacturing.\nTransport of Processed Goods\n: The processed goods, such as paper rolls, are loaded onto trucks and transported again to the market. This step is part of the\nTertiary Sector\n(transportation services).\nProduction of Finished Goods\n: The processed materials are further refined into final products like notebooks or printed materials. This also belongs to the\nSecondary Sector\n, as it's part of manufacturing.\nRetail and Sale\n: Finally, the finished products are displayed and sold in shops, where consumers can buy them. This is a\nTertiary Sector\nactivity as it involves trade and services.\nEach stage in the process shows how the primary, secondary, and tertiary sectors are interconnected, with raw materials being transformed and delivered as final products to consumers. You can discuss how each sector plays a vital role in this chain of production with your classmates.\nLET\u2019S EXPLORE\nLabel the pictures in the illustration shown in Fig. 14.1 on page 206 as:"
